diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk report that router16 has crashed and Campus 1 (172.16.10.x / 20.x / 30.x) is unreachable is not supported by any live evidence. A full four-phase broad search was conducted.

**Phase 1 (reachability):** All 28 sampled pairs returned 0% loss and status "ok", including cross-campus paths from external hosts (dc_pc5 10.5.0.5, pc1 10.5.0.1) directly to Campus 1 hosts (172.16.10.8, 172.16.20.9). Traffic is flowing normally.

**Phase A (L2/infrastructure):** infra_sweep flagged 0 devices — no ACL rules, no ARP poisoning, no addressing anomalies, all 15 hosts with valid IPs and default routes. l2_snapshot found 0 duplicate MACs; the DOWN interfaces flagged belong exclusively to `unused_*` collision domains that are deliberately unconnected in the topology (baseline behaviour).

**Phase B (control plane):** ospf_snapshot confirms router16 is fully operational: ospfd is running and responsive (vtysh ok, both control sockets present), it holds two Full OSPF adjacencies (bond0→mswitch0 as DR, eth2→router7 as Backup), carries 18 OSPF routes, and is advertising its prefixes correctly. mswitch0 similarly holds a Full adjacency back to router16 and redistributes the 172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, and 172.16.30.0/24 subnets into the OSPF domain. tc_snapshot found no traffic shaping anywhere.

**Phase D (service/resource):** pressure_sweep found 0 suspect devices — no stress tools, no CPU spikes, no socket exhaustion on any of the 28 devices.

**Root cause:** No fault exists. router16 is alive and forwarding; Campus 1 is fully reachable. The helpdesk report is a false alarm.

**Fix:** No corrective action is needed. Do **not** reboot router16 — doing so would cause a real outage on a currently healthy device.
=== END ===